{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.95,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["U9", "U25"],
      "pins": ["U9.4", "U9.5", "U25.4", "U25.5"],
      "nets": ["GND"],
      "observed": "U9 and U25 are isolated CAN transceivers (CA-IS3050G / ISO1050DUB). Their GND1 (pin 4) and GND2 (pin 5) are both connected to the same GND net.",
      "expected": "GND1 and GND2 should be on separate isolated ground nets to maintain galvanic isolation between the logic side and the CAN bus side.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(node (ref \"U9\") (pin \"4\") (pinfunction \"GND1\") (pintype \"power_in\")) ... (node (ref \"U9\") (pin \"5\") (pinfunction \"GND2\") (pintype \"power_in\"))"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "The isolation barrier is shorted, defeating the isolated CAN bus design. This can cause ground loops, noise, and damage to the transceivers or connected equipment.",
      "confidence": 0.95
    },
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["R75"],
      "pins": ["R75.1", "R75.2"],
      "nets": ["VOUTP", "VOUTN"],
      "observed": "R75 has value '24k' but its description says '24Ω' and its MPN is 'FRC0805J240TSD' (a 24Ω 5% resistor).",
      "expected": "R75 should be a 24kΩ resistor, as required for a bleeder across the PoE rectified output (VOUTP/VOUTN).",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(comp (ref \"R75\") (value \"24k\") ... (description \" 125mW Thick Film Resistors 150V ±100ppm/℃ ±5% 24Ω 0805 Chip Resistor - Surface Mount ROHS\") ... (field (name \"MPN\") \"FRC0805J240TSD\")"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "If assembled with the MPN, a 24Ω resistor across the ~48V PoE rail would dissipate ~96W, likely causing overheating, fire, or power supply failure.",
      "confidence": 0.95
    },
    {
      "category": "short_to_ground",
      "components": ["U34", "U35"],
      "pins": ["U34.9", "U35.9"],
      "nets": ["GND"],
      "observed": "U34 and U35 (SC16IS752 dual UARTs) have pin 9 (I2C/~SPI) tied to GND. Their pins 10-13 are wired for SPI mode (A0/~CS, A1/SI, SO, SCL/SCLK).",
      "expected": "For SPI mode, pin 9 must be tied high (to VDD, e.g., NET0126 or +3V3). Tying it to GND selects I2C mode, which is incompatible with the SPI wiring and would short SDA (pin 14) to GND.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(node (ref \"U34\") (pin \"9\") (pinfunction \"I2C/~{SPI}\") (pintype \"input\")) on net GND; (node (ref \"U34\") (pin \"10\") (pinfunction \"A0/~{CS}\") ...) on NET0210; (node (ref \"U34\") (pin \"11\") (pinfunction \"A1/SI\") ...) on NET0145; (node (ref \"U34\") (pin \"12\") (pinfunction \"SO\") ...) on NET0200; (node (ref \"U34\") (pin \"13\") (pinfunction \"SCL/SCLK\") ...) on NET0024"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "The UARTs will not function in the intended SPI mode. If forced into I2C mode, pin 14 (SDA) is tied to GND, shorting the I2C data line and preventing communication.",
      "confidence": 0.9
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The design files contain multiple anomalies: the isolated CAN transceivers have their isolated grounds shorted together, R75 has a dangerous value mismatch (24k vs 24Ω), and the SC16IS752 UARTs are configured for I2C mode while wired for SPI. These would cause loss of isolation, potential fire, and non-functional UARTs."
}

